7) = Phi((1.7-1.8)/0.66) = Phi(-0.15) = 0.44 of total. P(core < 2.0 | growth < 1.7) is crushed by the same composition floor: a genuine BIS-style demand-shortfall world with underlying G7 core at 1.5% still PRINTS ~1.9-2.0% on the PPP-weighted OECD-38 aggregate, so 0.20 under PPP construction and 0.45 under market weights, blended 0.70(0.20) + 0.30(0.45) = 0.140 + 0.135 = 0.275. D = 44 x 0.275 = 12.1 -> 12. STEP 4, B = residual = 100 - 11 - 33 - 12 = 44. SUM CHECK: 11 + 44 + 33 + 12 = 100 exactly. CONSISTENCY CHECKS PERFORMED. (i) Against my CQ3: P50 of 1.8% lies inside B's 1.7-2.5 band and only 0.1pp above D's threshold, so B must carry the plurality and does (44), and A at 11 is at or below the 11.3% my own sigma implies — the error is in the conservative direction, never the permissive one. (ii) Against my CQ2: C requires >=2.70% on the shorter, later 2032-36 window, strictly tighter than CQ2 conjunct A's >=2.50% on 2027-36 whose marginal I set at 0.68; C's 33 is comfortably below 68, and C is approximately a subset of that conjunct because a 2032-36 average >=2.70% would drag the 2027-36 average above 2.50% given elevated 2026-28 inertia. C = 33 is also below my full CQ2 answer of 46 only by coincidence of the independent China leg, which I do not rely on. (iii) C and D are disjoint by construction (core >=2.7 versus core <2.0) and both are evaluated after A, so no double-counting. (iv) B absorbs ~17pp of the low-growth/moderate-inflation leftover cell (growth <2.0 with core 2.0-2.69) by rule 4 plus ~27pp from its own literal band; a naive prose reading of the labels would have split that 17pp between C and D and produced roughly C 42 / D 20 / B 27. (v) My distribution differs from all three Wave-1 panelists chiefly in C (33 versus 17/23/30), and the entire gap is the OECD-38 PPP index-composition arithmetic, not a difference about the real economy.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:51.696Z","se